1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,540
In 2014, in an operating room in St.

2
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:08,840
Louis, a nerve surgeon named Susan
Mackinnon was looking for one small

3
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:10,410
nerve in a patient's knee.

4
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:14,770
The patient had lived with pain for
years, after several operations.

5
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:17,910
The nerve wasn't where the
textbooks said it would be.

6
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:24,740
Her colleague, Andrew Yee, went to an
old anatomy atlas, photographed

7
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:29,290
one of its paintings, and sent the
picture into the operating room.

8
00:00:29,740 --> 00:00:32,070
With it, they found the nerve.

9
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:34,050
The patient got better.

10
00:00:35,670 --> 00:00:42,110
The atlas was made in Vienna, between
1937 and 1960, by an anatomist

11
00:00:42,130 --> 00:00:45,080
named Eduard Pernkopf and a
team of painters.

12
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:48,700
Many surgeons will tell you there
has never been a better one.

13
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:54,060
During the war, Pernkopf's institute
received the bodies of at least

14
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:59,440
thirteen hundred and seventy-seven
people executed by the Nazi regime,

15
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:02,120
most of them for political reasons.

16
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:03,950
Some of the paintings were
made in those years.

17
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:09,310
No one can say whose body
that page showed.

18
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:12,990
No one in any of the paintings
has ever been identified.

19
00:01:16,970 --> 00:01:18,190
And the patient in St.

20
00:01:18,210 --> 00:01:22,320
Louis wasn't told, at the time,
where the picture came from.

21
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:27,010
So, should surgeons use it?

22
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:31,740
If a book holds knowledge that came
from people who were killed, and

23
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:37,300
never agreed, and that knowledge can
heal someone today, is it right

24
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:42,130
to use it? And if it is, what do
we owe the people in it?
